{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Lake Osborne, Florida, USA is a 378-acre (152.9 hectares) lake that is part of a system of once natural freshwater lakes lying along the western slope of the coastal ridge in Palm Beach County just west of the Florida Intracoastal Waterway and Atlantic Ocean.","title":"Lake Osborne"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is located within the C-16 drainage basin which occupies approximately 40,031 acres of land (16,200 hectares).","title":"Lake Osborne"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Five drainage canals discharge directly to Lake Osborne, and Lake Osborne discharges via the E-4 canal to the C16 and C51 canals to the Lake Worth Lagoon.","title":"Lake Osborne"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The lake is bordered on the west by John Prince Memorial Park, and on the east be the City of Lake Worth.","title":"Lake Osborne"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Lake Worth Lagoon is a lagoon located in Palm Beach County, Florida.","title":"Lake Worth Lagoon"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It runs parallel to the coast, and is separated from the Atlantic Ocean by barrier beaches, including Palm Beach Island.","title":"Lake Worth Lagoon"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The lagoon is connected to the Atlantic Ocean by two permanent, man-made inlets.","title":"Lake Worth Lagoon"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Lake Worth is a city in Palm Beach County, Florida, United States, which takes its name from the body of water along its eastern border, originally called "Lake Worth", and now generally known as the Lake Worth Lagoon.","title":"Lake Worth, Florida"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The lake itself was named for General William J. Worth, who led U.S. forces during the last part of the Second Seminole War.","title":"Lake Worth, Florida"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" s of 2010 , the population estimated by the U.S. Census Bureau was 34,910.","title":"Lake Worth, Florida"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The city is included in the Miami-Fort Lauderdale-Pompano Beach Metropolitan Area, which is home to approximately 5,563,857 people.","title":"Lake Worth, Florida"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Cascade Locks State Airport (IATA: CZK, ICAO: KCZK, FAA LID: CZK) is a public use airport located one nautical mile (2 km) northeast of the central business district of Cascade Locks, a city in Hood River County, Oregon, United States.","title":"Cascade Locks State Airport"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is owned by the Oregon Department of Aviation.","title":"Cascade Locks State Airport"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The South Lake Worth Inlet, also known as the Boynton Inlet, is an artificial cut through a barrier beach connecting the south end of the Lake Worth Lagoon in Palm Beach County, Florida with the Atlantic Ocean.","title":"South Lake Worth Inlet"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The inlet is 130 ft wide and 6 ft and 12 ft deep.","title":"South Lake Worth Inlet"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Palm Beach Inlet, also known as the Lake Worth Inlet is an artificial cut through a barrier island connecting the northern part of the Lake Worth Lagoon in Palm Beach County, Florida with the Atlantic Ocean.","title":"Lake Worth Inlet"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is bordered by the town of Palm Beach on the south, and by the town of Palm Beach Shores to the north.","title":"Lake Worth Inlet"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The inlet is also the entrance channel for the Port of Palm Beach.","title":"Lake Worth Inlet"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Its coordinates are .","title":"Lake Worth Inlet"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Cascade Locks (and Canal) is farther west.
